// Title: Windows Hypervisor Enforced Code Integrity Disabled
// Sigma ID: 8b7273a4-ba5d-4d8a-b04f-11f2900d043a
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems), Anish Bogati
// Date: 2023-03-14 | Modified: 2024-07-05
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1685
// Description: Detects changes to the HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity registry key and the "Enabled" value
//   being set to 0 in order to disable the Hypervisor Enforced Code Integrity feature. This allows
//   an attacker to load unsigned and untrusted code to be run in the kernel
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate system administration tasks that require disabling HVCI for troubleshooting purposes when certain drivers or applications are incompatible with it.
// References:
//   - https://www.welivesecurity.com/2023/03/01/blacklotus-uefi-bootkit-myth-confirmed/
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/04e487c1828d76df3e834621f4f893ea756d5232/atomics/T1562.001/T1562.001.md#atomic-test-43---disable-hypervisor-enforced-code-integrity-hvci
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_deviceguard_hypervisorenforcedcodeintegrity_disabled.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (RegistryKey endswith "\\Control\\DeviceGuard\\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity" or RegistryKey endswith "\\Control\\DeviceGuard\\Scenarios\\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\\Enabled" or RegistryKey endswith "\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity") and RegistryValueData =~ "DWORD (0x00000000)"
